  "config": {
    "name": "flowconx_app_task",
    "description": "The same architecture, the same split, the same budget -- classifying the application instead of the service category.\nThis exists because the enrollment result forced the question. On the identical frozen embedding, with identical k and procedure, prototype enrollment reaches macro-F1 0.7378 identifying the application from a single labelled flow and 0.4234 for the service category. That is not a small difference in difficulty; it says the embedding encodes application identity and does not encode the service taxonomy it is being scored against.\nThe 5G service taxonomy is the dataset's own directory structure, and it groups applications that behave nothing alike: `metaverse` is Zepeto and Roblox, `game_streaming` is GeForce Now and KT GameBox. A model that fingerprints applications well will look bad on it, and a coarse volume statistic that captures \"bursty and small-packet\" will look good -- which is exactly the pattern in the headline table, where XGBoost on thirteen flow scalars beats us by 0.25.\nIf this run scores well, the paper's task should change and the service results become a documented negative. If it does not, the architecture has a problem that reframing will not fix. Either answer is worth one run.\nNote that 15 applications with `min_class_count: 100` includes apps whose captures land entirely in test under session-disjoint splitting (roblox has one capture file). Those are unpredictable by construction and will drag macro-F1 down; the per-class table in the result JSON separates them out.\n",
    "seed": 2,
    "output_root": "results",
    "data": {
      "dataset": "fiveg_traffic",
      "csv": "data/processed/fiveg_traffic.csv",
      "label_column": "app",
      "split_protocol": "session_disjoint",
      "split_seed": 42,
      "val_fraction": 0.1,
      "test_fraction": 0.2,
      "rare_class_mode": "drop",
